# SRG-APP-000830-AU-000290  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must for password-based authentication, maintain a
# list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an
# organization-defined frequency.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000830-AU-000290
group:           V-263574
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   ORGANIZATIONAL
grade:           D
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-004058
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    None toward the list itself: Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2 has no configuration
                 setting that holds, references, or maintains a list of commonly used,
                 expected, or compromised passwords. What the product can do about weak
                 passwords is enforce composition rules and reject reuse in
                 authentication.conf [splunk_auth] (minPasswordLength,
                 minPasswordUppercase, minPasswordLowercase, minPasswordDigit,
                 minPasswordSpecial, enablePasswordHistory, passwordHistoryCount); those
                 are assessed under SRG-APP-000845 and SRG-APP-000870 and are not a deny
                 list.

external:        The list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords and the
                 frequency at which it is maintained - carried by organizational policy.
external:        Screening of passwords against that list - carried by the enterprise
                 directory service or identity provider (for example an Active Directory
                 password filter) where authentication.conf authType is LDAP, SAML, or
                 ProxySSO.
external:        For native Splunk accounts, no screening component exists in the product,
                 so compliance for those accounts rests on the organization's
                 account-management procedure.

manual_reason:   No conf key in the product expresses a password deny list or the frequency
                 at which it is maintained, so no observed configuration value can decide
                 this requirement; the frequency is organization-defined in any case.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/authentication.conf.spec:437-600 ([splunk_auth]:
                 complexity, history, expiration and lockout settings only; no deny-list
                 setting in the stanza)
finding:         Not a finding against the application: Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2 has no
                 setting that maintains a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised
                 passwords, so this requirement is carried by the organization's policy and
                 by the directory service or identity provider that screens passwords; this
                 determination is from the perspective of the application layer alone and
                 the status must be recorded from those components.
